1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,850
Put just 23 people in a room,

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:05,340
and there's almost a 50% chance
two share the same birthday!

3
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:06,920
Sounds impossible, right?

4
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:09,500
There are 365 days in a year.

5
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:12,160
Surely you'd need hundreds
of people to get a match.

6
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:13,780
But the mathematics says otherwise.

7
00:00:14,170 --> 00:00:14,960
Here's the trick.

8
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:18,280
We're not asking whether
someone shares YOUR birthday.

9
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:21,260
We're checking whether ANY two
people in the room share a birthday.

10
00:00:21,570 --> 00:00:26,800
With 23 people, there are already 253
different pairs of people who could match.

11
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:31,860
The probability that everyone has a
different birthday is only about 49.3%.

12
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:35,280
Which means the probability of at least
one shared birthday is approximately

13
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:36,870
50.7%!

14
00:00:37,380 --> 00:00:40,810
And with 50 people,
that probability jumps to about 97%.

15
00:00:41,670 --> 00:00:43,660
This is called the Birthday Paradox.

16
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:46,900
So tell me, how many people are
in your office or classroom?

17
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,770
You might already have a birthday match!

